Z||000000||b lo 0||08-01-2001||01:28 AM||||I'm thinking 3 8" subs, one behind each seat im custom boxes (my design) and one in a genuine AusFiero center console.  1 amp per speaker, mounted above the two on the firewall, one for the center under the passenger seat (for symmetricality (is that a word?)).  Whaddyallthink?  I think 3 8's in an area the size of a Fiero would hit HARD.  Luckily, I have my store back online, so I can get the stuff at cost.  I'll put the link in my sig, but in the meantime,<BR> <A HREF="http://www.audioimagery.vstoreauto.com" TARGET=_blank>www.audioimagery.vstoreauto.com</A> <P>------------------<BR><FONT SIZE="1">'85 GT (in progress)<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.angelfire.com/ga3/audioimagery/ice.gif">      <BR>White Lightnin!  (NOT moonshine, for all you hillbillies!)</FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000001||Philphine||08-01-2001||02:04 AM||||i've been trying to work out something similar for about a year. i have an 8" jl 8w1 in my passeger footwell. i want to try 3 8w0 12ohms. 1 in the footwell and 1 in each door. the subs have been on back order all this time.||||reg||1
Z||000002||artherd||08-01-2001||05:13 AM||||I'm going to try to keep the language simple, acoustic termonology can quickly become hard to follow :<P>Two *very* good 8 or 7" drivers operating in true stereo on either side of the car will produce essentially liniar responce above 130db down to 25hz in the fiero. (that is loud :)  What is critical is liniarity and crosover componentry.  You have a nice natural ~12db boost around 100hz, this can create unpleasant "boombing" which sounds loud, but is not true bass. (and it gives me a headache :)  <P>The best way to handle it is in digital filters based on actual calibrated microphone readings take in the car itself (with occupants)  however, you can get close just fudging things if you are careful and use the built-in closed-loop feedback measurment devices on either side of your head.  Come to think of it, use that middle thing a lot too :)<P>I use a set of FOCAL (French made reference drive units, excellent quality. They make a car line too, but I use only their studio line.)  7" kevlar woffers, I'm still playing with their locations, and that of the tweters (and I haven't even picked a midbass yet, though I will probally go with their 5" polyglass or neoglass in the stock locations. Maybe 6" polykevlar.<P>It's really mind-bogglingly easy to get GREAT sound in a fiero, all you need is to abandon *every* street notion about car audio :)<P>Too much woffer will destroy everything, the focus needs to be on mid and upper range quality, soundstaging, reflection and absorbtion in a very small padded space.<P>Eventually I will have much more on this matter, I'm still barely getting started on the fiero's acoustics (I'm too busy getting rid of the damn pushrod motors :P)<P>Ben!<BR>Ben.<P><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000003||Robert||08-01-2001||05:27 AM||||You might want to take a look at Fierosound’s site <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fierosound/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/fierosound/</A> <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||b lo 0||08-02-2001||01:22 PM||||Atherd, I've been installing for about 2 years, including custom box design, so the terminology is not completely alien.  I agree, too much bass will ruin a good stereo, but I've got 2 4x10 triax's going in the dash, with 4 3.5 coax's going in the headrests.  I don't think 8 tweeters and six mids will have a problem keeping up with 3 8" subs.  I found a 3 channel amp, so I think I'll go with that instead of 3 separate amps.  I had a Nissan Pulsar with a Sony head unit and changer, 6 Excalibur tweets, 2 4" mids up front, and 2 6.5 midbass's in the back, with a JL Audio 12W6 running off a Prestige 400w bridged amp, and it sounded great.  I only hope to get that quality in a Fiero.  BTW, I've seen fierosound's site, damn nice system, I'm just going for practical, semi-stealth install (only one sub visible, all subs in passenger compartment).||||reg||1
Z||000005||PearlBlueSoul||08-02-2001||05:14 PM||||if you're not really a bass-head and want bass that has some good range, I suggest a 12 in a truck box, mounted in the front trunk. <P>Had one in my MR2, worked great, was pu8shing prolly 125 watts RMS into the sub.  Filled in the range quite well I must say.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>1987 Toyota MR2 N/A 5 speed...and yes, it's modded  <IMG SRC="http://members.aol.com/mr2drs/images/naughty.gif"> <BR>  <A HREF="http://www.atlantamr2s.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.atlantamr2s.com</A>  <BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/darionscard" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/darionscard</A>  <BR>      <IMG SRC="http://members.aol.com/mr2drs/images/pa010022.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000006||87GTBro||08-02-2001||05:49 PM||||I mounted a 10" sub behind the passenger seat like fierosound being pushed by a 240 W California amp and an active crossover...<P>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/firewallsub.jpg">  <P>Oh yeah... got a Kenwood KDC-MP8017 MP3 player head unit...<P>I am planning on puting the 8" factory box up under the dash and run mids through it.  Should sound sweet!<P>------------------<BR>AOL IM: GTFiero86Dan<BR>'86 GT 4 speed<BR>'87 GT Auto<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/87GTBrosig1.gif">  <p>[This message has been edited by 87GTBro (edited 08-02-2001).]||||reg||1
